      "title": "Part IV · One change at a time",
      "chapters": [
        {
          "id": "37",
          "title": "Do the brains matter?",
          "file": "chapters/37-do-the-brains-matter.md",
          "experiment": "E07",
          "status": "analysed"
        },
        {
          "id": "38",
          "title": "How does a world's size follow its tokens?",
          "file": "chapters/38-how-does-a-worlds-size-follow-its-tokens.md",
          "experiment": "E08",
          "status": "analysed"
        },
        {
          "id": "39",
          "title": "How fast should brains change?",
          "file": "chapters/39-how-fast-should-brains-change.md",
          "experiment": "E09",
          "status": "ready"
        },
        {
          "id": "40",
          "title": "Brains replaced by their average",
          "note": "A better control than decisions by chance (Chapter 37): every decision drawn at random from the distribution of that decision in the settled baseline worlds — first unconditionally, then given the agent's own tokens and connections. If a world of such agents looks like the baseline, what the brains add is their average, not what they respond to."
        },
        {
          "id": "41",
          "title": "What keeps wealth spread?",
          "note": "No coalitions: allow_revolutions off, against the baseline runs of Chapter 10."
        },
        {
          "id": "42",
          "title": "Meta III",
          "note": "Written once Chapters 37 to 41 are analysed."
        },
        {
          "id": "43",
          "title": "How big should a brain be?",
          "note": "The hidden layers, against the baseline runs of Chapter 10."
        },
        {
          "id": "44",
          "title": "Does size change the dynamics?",
          "note": "Builds on Chapter 38: the wander, the sweeps and dying out, against the size of a world."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Part V · Towards open-ended evolution",
      "chapters": [
        {
          "id": "45",
          "title": "Mutation at replication, and nowhere else",
          "note": "The first of the mechanics in research/Research.md §4, once Part IV is done."
        },
        {
          "id": "46",
          "title": "Can a brain hold its place?",
          "note": "Incumbency: the agent on a node defends it with an advantage. How long brains then hold hubs, and how long partners last (Chapter 36)."
        },
        {
          "id": "47",
          "title": "Kin that know each other",
          "note": "Heritable tags every agent can see on its neighbours. Kin discrimination and Hamilton's accounting of Chapter 34, against the baseline."
        },
        {
          "id": "48",
          "title": "When cooperation produces",
          "note": "Mutual flow yield: a connection used both ways in a game makes new tokens, with a small decay of every holding. research/Research.md §4.4."
        },
        {
          "id": "49",
          "title": "An open world",
          "note": "Connections proposed beyond the neighbourhood, and pieces that separate allowed to live and meet again (Chapters 31, 32 and 35)."
        },
        {
          "id": "50",
          "title": "Meta IV",
          "note": "What moved on the ladder of Chapter 1, written once Chapters 45 to 49 are analysed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Part VI · Towards a physics: space, scale and locality",
      "chapters": [
        {
          "id": "51",
          "title": "Worlds of 100,000 tokens",
          "file": "chapters/51-worlds-of-100000-tokens.md",
          "experiment": "E10",
          "status": "ready"
        },
        {
          "id": "52",
          "title": "Dimensions at scale",
          "note": "The measures of Chapter 23 on the worlds of 100,000 tokens (Chapter 51): does a world look three-dimensional only at middle scales, as causal dynamical triangulations look four-dimensional only at large ones? Ball growth and the spectral dimension as functions of scale, and the width of worlds from 3,200 to 409,600 tokens (Chapter 38)."
        },
        {
          "id": "53",
          "title": "Which properties have no scale?",
          "note": "The same quantities measured at many sizes and rescaled: do their distributions collapse onto one curve? Starts from the cuts of Chapter 38, whose share of a world has the same distribution at every size."
        },
        {
          "id": "54",
          "title": "Local rules only",
          "note": "The three rules that reach across a world, each made local: a piece that separates lives on, the tokens of the dead go to their former neighbours, and every agent draws its random numbers from a stream of its own (Chapter 7)."
        },
        {
          "id": "55",
          "title": "How far does a difference travel?",
          "note": "Twin worlds that differ in one token at one agent: how fast the difference spreads and whether it grows or dies away — the light cone of Chapter 7, measured. Needs the local random numbers of Chapter 54."
        },
        {
          "id": "56",
          "title": "A source and a sink",
          "note": "A world with a flow running through it: tokens that are spent when used and slowly renewed, or a few nodes that release tokens and a way for tokens to leave. What forms in the flow? (Chapter 7; research/Research.md §4.3.)"
        },
        {
          "id": "57",
          "title": "Particles",
          "note": "Patterns that persist while what carries them changes: tokens bound in loops, groups that outlast their members, local beats in the flow of tokens (Chapter 7)."
        },
        {
          "id": "58",
          "title": "One agent in a sea of average agents",
          "note": "A large world of agents that decide as the average evolved agent does (Chapter 40), and in it a single agent, or a small group, with a brain of its own: can it draw tokens from the sea without exhausting it or being cut off, can its line invade, can a group hold together? Invasion analysis and a reservoir, in one experiment."
        },
        {
          "id": "59",
          "title": "Meta V",
          "note": "What the measurements say about space, scale and matter, written once Chapters 51 to 58 are analysed."
        }
      ]
    },
